WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:08.526
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.826 --> 00:00:17.086
Unit three: The Ethics of AI. In this unit, you will debate the risks and responsibilities of artificial intelligence.

4
00:00:18.083 --> 00:00:19.653
algorithmic bias.

5
00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:26.649
algorithmic bias means unfair results produced by a computer system because of biased data.

6
00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:31.479
Algorithmic bias led the hiring tool to reject female applicants.

7
00:00:32.179 --> 00:00:36.271
Your turn. Say: algorithmic bias.

8
00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:40.704
algorithmic bias.

9
00:00:41.717 --> 00:00:43.240
accountability gap.

10
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:49.864
accountability gap means a situation where nobody can be held responsible for a harmful decision.

11
00:00:50.164 --> 00:00:54.903
When an AI makes a mistake, there is often an accountability gap.

12
00:00:55.603 --> 00:00:59.080
Your turn. Say: accountability gap.

13
00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:03.257
accountability gap.

14
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.715
autonomous weapon.

15
00:01:06.415 --> 00:01:11.631
autonomous weapon means a weapon that can choose and attack targets without human control.

16
00:01:11.931 --> 00:01:14.975
Many scientists want to ban autonomous weapons.

17
00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:19.082
Your turn. Say: autonomous weapon.

18
00:01:21.782 --> 00:01:23.225
autonomous weapon.

19
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:25.682
explainability.

20
00:01:26.382 --> 00:01:31.400
explainability means the ability to explain how an AI reached its decision.

21
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.393
Banks must ensure explainability when AI rejects a loan.

22
00:01:37.093 --> 00:01:39.839
Your turn. Say: explainability.

23
00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:43.934
explainability.

24
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.288
ethical framework.

25
00:01:46.988 --> 00:01:51.483
ethical framework means a set of principles that guide right and wrong decisions.

26
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:55.722
The company published an ethical framework for its AI products.

27
00:01:56.422 --> 00:01:59.492
Your turn. Say: ethical framework.

28
00:02:02.192 --> 00:02:03.402
ethical framework.

29
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:05.952
data exploitation.

30
00:02:06.652 --> 00:02:11.647
data exploitation means using people's data unfairly for profit.

31
00:02:11.947 --> 00:02:16.083
Critics accuse social networks of data exploitation.

32
00:02:16.783 --> 00:02:20.805
Your turn. Say: data exploitation.

33
00:02:23.505 --> 00:02:25.261
data exploitation.

34
00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.623
machine ethics.

35
00:02:28.323 --> 00:02:32.783
machine ethics means the study of how machines should make moral decisions.

36
00:02:33.083 --> 00:02:37.277
Self-driving cars raise difficult questions of machine ethics.

37
00:02:37.977 --> 00:02:41.442
Your turn. Say: machine ethics.

38
00:02:44.142 --> 00:02:45.596
machine ethics.

39
00:02:46.583 --> 00:02:47.968
moral agency.

40
00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:53.651
moral agency means the ability to make moral choices and be responsible for them.

41
00:02:53.951 --> 00:02:56.508
Can a machine ever have moral agency?

42
00:02:57.208 --> 00:03:00.406
Your turn. Say: moral agency.

43
00:03:03.106 --> 00:03:04.432
moral agency.

44
00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:07.602
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:07.902 --> 00:03:12.247
Rithy, should AI be allowed to make decisions about people's lives?

46
00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:18.780
Only with strict limits. Algorithmic bias has already caused serious harm.

47
00:03:20.083 --> 00:03:21.084
Such as?

48
00:03:22.383 --> 00:03:27.030
Hiring systems that rejected women because the training data came mostly from men.

49
00:03:28.317 --> 00:03:32.443
And who is responsible when that happens? There's a real accountability gap.

50
00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:39.131
Exactly. Had the company tested the system properly, the problem would have been found.

51
00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:43.403
Should companies be forced to guarantee explainability?

52
00:03:44.717 --> 00:03:49.958
Definitely. Were I a regulator, I would require it for every high-risk system.

53
00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:54.712
Grammar focus: Inversion in conditionals.

54
00:03:55.012 --> 00:03:59.322
In formal English, we can drop if and invert the subject and auxiliary.

55
00:03:59.622 --> 00:04:05.894
Had they tested means if they had tested; were I means if I were; should you need means if you need.

56
00:04:06.194 --> 00:04:10.562
For example: Had they tested the system, they would have found the bias.

57
00:04:10.862 --> 00:04:16.798
Inverted conditionals drop 'if' and are common in formal writing, reports and speeches.

58
00:04:17.783 --> 00:04:23.048
Now read and listen. Who Is Responsible When AI Gets It Wrong?.

59
00:04:23.348 --> 00:04:28.691
Artificial intelligence now helps decide who gets a loan, a job or even medical treatment.

60
00:04:29.983 --> 00:04:35.396
These systems learn from data, and if the data is biased, the results will be biased too.

61
00:04:36.683 --> 00:04:43.907
In one famous case, a recruitment tool learned to prefer male candidates because past hires were mostly men.

62
00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:50.815
When such mistakes occur, it is often unclear who should be held accountable.

63
00:04:52.117 --> 00:04:56.438
Is it the programmer, the company, or the users who trusted the system?

64
00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:04.916
Many experts argue that explainability should be a legal requirement for high-risk AI.

65
00:05:06.217 --> 00:05:11.955
Others warn that autonomous weapons represent the most dangerous accountability gap of all.

66
00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:18.059
Should these issues remain unresolved, public trust in technology may collapse.

67
00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:21.956
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:22.256 --> 00:05:26.461
Unfair results caused by biased data are called blank.

69
00:05:26.761 --> 00:05:32.226
A, machine ethics. B, algorithmic bias. C, moral agency.

70
00:05:36.217 --> 00:05:39.261
The answer is B: algorithmic bias.

71
00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:44.526
blank we known about the bias, we would have stopped the project.

72
00:05:44.826 --> 00:05:47.527
A, If. B, Had. C, Were.

73
00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.946
The answer is B: Had.

74
00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:58.100
blank I in charge, I would change the policy.

75
00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.264
A, Were. B, Had. C, Should.

76
00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:06.681
The answer is A: Were.

77
00:06:08.183 --> 00:06:12.215
The ability to explain how an AI reached its decision is blank.

78
00:06:12.515 --> 00:06:19.372
A, explainability. B, data exploitation. C, ethical framework.

79
00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:26.219
The answer is A: explainability.

80
00:06:27.717 --> 00:06:31.539
According to the text, why did the recruitment tool prefer men?

81
00:06:31.839 --> 00:06:37.768
A, it was programmed to. B, past hires were mostly men. C, women applied less.

82
00:06:41.783 --> 00:06:45.699
The answer is B: past hires were mostly men.

83
00:06:47.183 --> 00:06:53.342
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should there be international laws controlling artificial intelligence?

84
00:06:58.642 --> 00:07:03.985
Part two: Give your position, two reasons, and one example of a risk.

85
00:07:10.285 --> 00:07:28.010
Yes, I strongly believe so. Firstly, AI crosses borders, so national laws alone are not enough; secondly, the risks are too serious to leave to companies. Autonomous weapons, for example, could make life-or-death decisions without any human control.

86
00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:30.751
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:37.119
Great work today! You learned eight new words and inversion in conditionals.

88
00:07:37.419 --> 00:07:41.848
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

89
00:07:42.148 --> 00:07:44.591
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:44.891 --> 00:07:47.520
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

